ed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-10\\sin(t),\\,4\\cos(t)⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }10\\cos(t)\\cdot 4\\cos(t)\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }40\\cos^{2}(t)\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8644454b-ee25-59fd-ae65-9770a0b44442" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Bounded by a Closed Curve Using Green’s Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f4a1110-34fc-5148-a06f-96646119ba61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 72 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 48 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522942} {:answer/id #uuid "3f3bdd1f-89e4-5d99-bc21-dfa17ad588d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 9 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 6 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522946} {:answer/id #uuid "5169bc62-4492-5bdf-b8a8-6d2f84ea9544" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 12 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 12 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 12 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522953} {:answer/id #uuid "9c99c8aa-da9b-5733-98eb-0e6ec4d6b9a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 36 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 24 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522982} {:answer/id #uuid "c12808f6-237f-5b1f-86c7-3023f50a79af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} + 36 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} - 24 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c99c8aa-da9b-5733-98eb-0e6ec4d6b9a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)} - 36 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 24 \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522982} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a97a502-7cc1-5fd9-bd8a-0e28fefbb90b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a3bed103-88e7-5529-bf46-ac64ed093a94" :question/problem "![](images/de/de9f562dd470e2377e39a7f604b34ca044a4da158ddb0877757b65387c3e2003.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨4(\\sin(t)-\\sin(2t)),\\,3(\\cos(t)-\\cos(2t))⟩$ for $t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}].$ \nThe area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the pointwise integrand obtained from the given $P$, $Q$, and parametrization using Green's theorem, with the sign adjusted for the orientation of $C$.\n\n*Hint: Note that $C$ is negatively oriented.*" :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since the path is negatively oriented.\n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨4(\\cos(t)-2\\cos(2t)),\\,3(-\\sin(t)+2\\sin(2t))⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}-x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}4(-\\sin(t)+\\sin(2t))\\cdot 3(-\\sin(t)+2\\sin(2t))\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{5\\pi /3}12(\\sin(2t)-\\sin(t))(2\\sin(2t)-\\sin(t))\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Area Bounded by a Closed Curve Using Green’s Theorem"}]}
